WEBVTT
NOTE how-does-tempered-glass-shatter - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.622
Tempered glass shatters into hundreds of
small pieces because its surface is under

00:00:05.622 --> 00:00:11.449
permanent compression, typically 69 to 103
megapascals according to ASTM C1048, while

00:00:11.449 --> 00:00:13.232
its core is under tension.

00:00:13.232 --> 00:00:18.594
Once a crack reaches the interior, the
stored elastic energy drives branching at

00:00:18.594 --> 00:00:23.822
speeds approaching 1500 meters per second
in soda-lime glass, as documented by

00:00:23.822 --> 00:00:26.101
fracture mechanics studies at MIT.

00:00:26.101 --> 00:00:27.988
The pattern you see is not random.

00:00:27.988 --> 00:00:31.429
It is the release of a stress field
engineered during cooling.

00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:33.867
How does tempered glass shatter?

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:39.502
The tempering process begins with annealed
glass heated to between 620 and 650

00:00:39.502 --> 00:00:44.487
degrees Celsius, above its glass
transition temperature but below its

00:00:44.487 --> 00:00:45.499
melting point.

00:00:45.499 --> 00:00:49.826
High-pressure air jets then cool the
surfaces rapidly while the interior

00:00:49.826 --> 00:00:51.328
remains hot and expanded.

00:00:51.328 --> 00:00:56.435
When the core finally cools, it contracts,
but the surfaces have already solidified.

00:00:56.435 --> 00:01:01.300
The result is a permanent state where the
surfaces are squeezed inward and the core

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:02.531
is stretched outward.

00:01:02.531 --> 00:01:05.502
This is not a coating or a treatment that
wears off.

00:01:05.502 --> 00:01:09.331
It is a frozen stress distribution
throughout the entire thickness.

00:01:09.331 --> 00:01:15.339
ASTM International specifies that the
surface compression in tempered glass must

00:01:15.339 --> 00:01:20.371
reach at least 69 megapascals, with
typical values extending to 103

00:01:20.371 --> 00:01:21.272
megapascals.

00:01:21.272 --> 00:01:26.120
The interior tension is lower in magnitude
but distributed through a greater volume.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:28.083
The two zones balance exactly.

00:01:28.083 --> 00:01:32.141
If they did not, the glass would curve or
warp during cooling.

00:01:32.141 --> 00:01:37.003
The stress field is in equilibrium, but it
is not stable against penetration.

00:01:37.003 --> 00:01:41.587
The compression layer typically extends
about one millimeter from each surface in

00:01:41.587 --> 00:01:45.832
a six-millimeter panel, leaving the
central four millimeters under tension.

00:01:45.832 --> 00:01:49.717
You can scratch the surface of tempered
glass without shattering it, as long as

00:01:49.717 --> 00:01:52.717
the scratch does not penetrate through the
compression layer.

00:01:52.717 --> 00:01:57.341
The compressive stress actively closes
surface cracks, preventing them from

00:01:57.341 --> 00:01:58.081
propagating.

00:01:58.081 --> 00:02:03.197
This is why tempered glass is stronger
than annealed glass under bending or

00:02:03.197 --> 00:02:07.871
The surface, where tensile stress would
normally initiate failure, is pre-loaded

00:02:07.871 --> 00:02:09.389
in the opposite direction.

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:14.431
A crack must reach the tensile core before
the stored energy becomes available for

00:02:14.431 --> 00:02:14.984
fracture.

00:02:14.984 --> 00:02:20.237
The transition from compression to tension
is not abrupt at the atomic scale, but it

00:02:20.237 --> 00:02:22.925
is functionally sharp in engineering
terms.

00:02:22.925 --> 00:02:24.724
The stress gradient is steep.

00:02:24.724 --> 00:02:29.935
Once a flaw breaches the compression zone
and enters the tension zone, the crack tip

00:02:29.935 --> 00:02:32.851
experiences a sudden increase in driving
force.

00:02:32.851 --> 00:02:36.433
The glass does not gradually weaken. It
switches states.

00:02:36.433 --> 00:02:41.358
The crack that was stable in compression
becomes unstable in tension, and the

00:02:41.358 --> 00:02:42.573
propagation begins.

00:02:42.573 --> 00:02:48.073
Crack propagation in soda-lime glass, the
material used in most architectural and

00:02:48.073 --> 00:02:52.147
automotive tempered glass, can reach 1500
meters per second.

00:02:52.147 --> 00:02:56.817
That is faster than the speed of sound in
air and roughly half the speed of sound in

00:02:56.817 --> 00:02:57.763
the glass itself.

00:02:57.763 --> 00:03:01.916
At these velocities, the crack does not
have time to find the path of least

00:03:01.916 --> 00:03:02.525
resistance.

00:03:02.525 --> 00:03:07.213
It moves nearly straight until it
encounters another crack or a boundary.

00:03:07.213 --> 00:03:10.954
The branching you see is not the crack
choosing directions.

00:03:10.954 --> 00:03:15.709
It is the crack splitting because it is
moving too fast to remain singular.

00:03:15.709 --> 00:03:20.056
The sequence begins when a sharp, hard
object penetrates the compression layer.

00:03:20.056 --> 00:03:25.843
A pointed impact, such as a ceramic chip
or a metal corner, concentrates force into

00:03:25.843 --> 00:03:26.749
a small area.

00:03:26.749 --> 00:03:31.543
Once the core is reached, the stored
tension drives the crack outward in all

00:03:31.543 --> 00:03:32.237
directions.

00:03:32.237 --> 00:03:37.075
The crack velocity is high enough that the
glass cannot dissipate energy smoothly.

00:03:37.075 --> 00:03:42.968
Instead, the crack front becomes unstable
and splits. Each branch can split again.

00:03:42.968 --> 00:03:48.214
The process continues until the stress
field is exhausted or the fragments are

00:03:48.214 --> 00:03:50.837
too small to support further branching.

00:03:50.837 --> 00:03:52.775
The branching is not chaotic.

00:03:52.775 --> 00:03:56.517
It follows the principles of dynamic
fracture mechanics.

00:03:56.517 --> 00:04:01.842
A crack moving at high speed in a brittle
material becomes unstable above a critical

00:04:01.842 --> 00:04:06.659
velocity, typically around 40 percent of
the material's Rayleigh wave speed.

00:04:06.659 --> 00:04:11.302
For soda-lime glass, that threshold is
crossed almost immediately once the core

00:04:11.302 --> 00:04:12.360
tension is tapped.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:15.835
The crack does not accelerate smoothly to
branching.

00:04:15.835 --> 00:04:18.107
It starts fast and branches early.

00:04:18.107 --> 00:04:21.842
The higher the residual stress, the
smaller the fragments.

00:04:21.842 --> 00:04:26.608
ASTM C1048 does not specify an exact
fragment size but provides a particle

00:04:26.608 --> 00:04:30.795
count test to verify that the glass has
been adequately tempered.

00:04:30.795 --> 00:04:35.750
In practice, fragments range from a few
millimeters to about ten millimeters on a

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:40.889
side, though the distribution is wide and
the published range extends from roughly 2

00:04:40.889 --> 00:04:43.397
to 15 millimeters depending on the study.

00:04:43.397 --> 00:04:47.714
The stress field determines how much
energy is available per unit volume.

00:04:47.714 --> 00:04:51.971
More energy means more branching events
before the cracks lose momentum.

00:04:51.971 --> 00:04:55.930
The relationship between stress and
fragment size is empirical.

00:04:55.930 --> 00:05:01.273
NIST fracture mechanics data show that the
energy release rate scales with the square

00:05:01.273 --> 00:05:02.781
of the stress intensity.

00:05:02.781 --> 00:05:07.727
Higher surface compression implies higher
core tension, which implies more stored

00:05:07.727 --> 00:05:08.643
elastic energy.

00:05:08.643 --> 00:05:11.697
When that energy is released, it must go
somewhere.

00:05:11.697 --> 00:05:14.152
It goes into creating new crack surfaces.

00:05:14.152 --> 00:05:17.293
The more surfaces created, the smaller the
pieces.

00:05:17.293 --> 00:05:22.256
The correlation is strong but not precise
enough to predict individual fragment

00:05:22.256 --> 00:05:22.947
dimensions.

00:05:22.947 --> 00:05:26.974
If you examine the fracture pattern
closely, you will see radial cracks

00:05:26.974 --> 00:05:31.227
emanating from the point of impact and
circumferential cracks forming rings

00:05:31.227 --> 00:05:31.795
around it.

00:05:31.795 --> 00:05:36.162
The radial cracks form first, driven by
the local stress concentration.

00:05:36.162 --> 00:05:41.021
The circumferential cracks form as the
surface compression tries to contain the

00:05:41.021 --> 00:05:42.189
expanding fracture.

00:05:42.189 --> 00:05:47.082
In the core, where tension dominates, the
pattern becomes more uniform, often

00:05:47.082 --> 00:05:48.861
described as a dice pattern.

00:05:48.861 --> 00:05:53.966
Individual fragment shapes are irregular
because crack paths interact in complex

00:05:53.966 --> 00:05:57.731
ways, but the overall scale is consistent
across the panel.

00:05:57.731 --> 00:06:01.514
The dice pattern is a signature of high
residual tension.

00:06:01.514 --> 00:06:06.692
Annealed glass, which has no residual
stress, fractures into a small number of

00:06:06.692 --> 00:06:08.285
large, irregular shards.

00:06:08.285 --> 00:06:13.252
Heat-strengthened glass, which has lower
residual stress than fully tempered glass,

00:06:13.252 --> 00:06:17.560
produces larger fragments than tempered
glass but smaller than annealed.

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:20.805
The fragment size is a direct readout of
the stress state.

00:06:20.805 --> 00:06:25.224
You can estimate how much energy was
stored by counting how many pieces result.

00:06:25.224 --> 00:06:30.166
The reason tempered glass is required in
car windows, shower doors, and building

00:06:30.166 --> 00:06:32.760
facades is not that it is harder to break.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:38.124
It is that when it does break, it produces
fragments with relatively low injury

00:06:38.124 --> 00:06:38.803
potential.

00:06:38.803 --> 00:06:44.507
Annealed glass fractures into long, sharp
shards that can cause deep lacerations.

00:06:44.507 --> 00:06:49.275
Tempered glass fractures into small,
blocky pieces with edges that are less

00:06:49.275 --> 00:06:50.610
likely to cut deeply.

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:54.869
The failure is more dramatic, but the
consequences are less severe.

00:06:54.869 --> 00:07:00.226
Tempered glass is also four to five times
stronger than annealed glass of the same

00:07:00.226 --> 00:07:04.080
thickness under bending loads, according
to ASTM standards.

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.618
The surface compression must be overcome
before tensile stress can develop at the

00:07:08.618 --> 00:07:10.859
surface, where cracks normally initiate.

00:07:10.859 --> 00:07:15.626
This makes tempered glass suitable for
applications where impact resistance is

00:07:15.626 --> 00:07:16.176
required.

00:07:16.176 --> 00:07:21.158
The trade-off is that once the compression
layer is breached, the entire panel fails

00:07:21.158 --> 00:07:21.632
at once.

00:07:21.632 --> 00:07:26.256
There is no partial failure. The glass is
either intact or shattered.

00:07:26.256 --> 00:07:30.432
I almost opened on the moment of impact,
because that's the dramatic beat.

00:07:30.432 --> 00:07:35.403
But impact doesn't decide the fracture
pattern — the cooling process already did,

00:07:35.403 --> 00:07:39.515
weeks earlier. I moved the real event to
where it actually happens.

00:07:39.515 --> 00:07:44.301
The edges of a tempered glass panel are
more vulnerable than the faces because the

00:07:44.301 --> 00:07:46.869
core tension is exposed at the cut
boundary.

00:07:46.869 --> 00:07:51.572
During manufacturing, the edges are often
ground or polished to remove flaws, but

00:07:51.572 --> 00:07:55.579
they cannot be placed under the same
compressive stress as the faces.

00:07:55.579 --> 00:08:01.137
A sharp impact to the edge can initiate a
crack that immediately enters the tension

00:08:01.137 --> 00:08:01.472
zone.

00:08:01.472 --> 00:08:06.381
This is why tempered glass panels are
often framed or protected at the edges in

00:08:06.381 --> 00:08:08.059
architectural applications.

00:08:08.059 --> 00:08:11.279
Edge protection is not cosmetic. It is
structural.

00:08:11.279 --> 00:08:16.367
A small chip at the edge can propagate
into the core with minimal force because

00:08:16.367 --> 00:08:19.136
there is no compression layer to arrest
it.

00:08:19.136 --> 00:08:23.525
The same chip on the face would likely be
harmless. The geometry matters.

00:08:23.525 --> 00:08:27.513
The stress distribution is
three-dimensional, and the edge is where

00:08:27.513 --> 00:08:31.680
that distribution is interrupted. The
interruption is a vulnerability.

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:34.825
Watch for the moment the stress field
becomes visible.

00:08:34.825 --> 00:08:39.308
In high-speed footage of tempered glass
fracture, you can see the crack front

00:08:39.308 --> 00:08:44.142
branching in real time, though it requires
frame rates in the tens of thousands per

00:08:44.142 --> 00:08:44.549
second.

00:08:44.549 --> 00:08:46.788
The pattern does not emerge gradually.

00:08:46.788 --> 00:08:51.029
It appears almost instantaneously because
the crack velocity is so high.

00:08:51.029 --> 00:08:55.547
The glass goes from intact to fully
fragmented in a few milliseconds.

00:08:55.547 --> 00:08:58.931
What you are seeing is not the glass
deciding how to break.

00:08:58.931 --> 00:09:03.405
You are seeing a pre-existing stress field
announcing itself through fracture.

00:09:03.405 --> 00:09:08.031
The exact depth of the compression layer
and the precise stress magnitudes vary

00:09:08.031 --> 00:09:11.603
with the tempering process, glass
composition, and thickness.

00:09:11.603 --> 00:09:16.928
Published values are representative, not
universal, and the range extends from

00:09:16.928 --> 00:09:21.912
about 0.5 to 3 millimeters depending on
panel thickness and cooling rate.

00:09:21.912 --> 00:09:26.424
The fracture pattern also depends on
factors like the location and geometry of

00:09:26.424 --> 00:09:29.491
the initiating flaw, which are not fully
predictable.

00:09:29.491 --> 00:09:34.829
What is well established is the mechanism:
residual stress drives rapid crack

00:09:34.829 --> 00:09:35.523
branching.

00:09:35.523 --> 00:09:40.281
The specific numbers for a given panel
require measurement, typically using

00:09:40.281 --> 00:09:42.629
polarimetry or surface stress meters.

00:09:42.629 --> 00:09:47.197
The general principle is consistent across
all thermally tempered glass.

00:09:47.197 --> 00:09:51.245
The shatter pattern of tempered glass is
not a failure of the material.

00:09:51.245 --> 00:09:54.153
It is the material performing exactly as
designed.

00:09:54.153 --> 00:09:58.572
The stress field that makes it strong is
the same stress field that makes it

00:09:58.572 --> 00:10:03.283
shatter completely. The engineering is in
choosing which failure mode you prefer.
